          %h3#factorization_properties Factorization properties
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong image factorization
            %p
              In a regular category, every morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : x\to y
              can be factored – uniquely up to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              – through its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coimage"} coimage
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi coim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} coim(f)
              as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi e
                    %mi coim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi i
                    %mi y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f : x \stackrel{e}{\to} coim(f) \stackrel{i}{\to} y
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphism"} regular epimorphism
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi coim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e : x \to coim(f)
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              coequalizes its kernel pair, there is a unique map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi coim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: coim(f) \to y
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %mi i
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = i e
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is monic and in fact represents the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coimage"} coimage
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              factors.
            %p This is the mere definition of first isomorphism theorem.
            %p
              A proof is spelled out on p. 30 of (
              %a{:href => "#vanOosten"}> vanOosten
              ).
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The classes of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphism"} regular epimorphism
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              form a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+factorization+system"} factorization system
          %h3#embedding_properties Embedding properties
          %p
          #barrembeddingtheorem.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              If a regular category is small, it admits particularly nice embeddings into presheaf categories. See
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr+embedding+theorem"} Barr embedding theorem
              for more.
          %h3#axiomatizability_properties Axiomatizability properties
          %p Roughly speaking, regular categories tend to be relatively well-behaved when it comes to desribing them in formalized logics.
          %h3#regular_functors_over_a_small_regular_category Regular functors over a small regular category
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Makkai)
              %br/
              If a regular category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℛ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
              is small, then the full subcategory of the functor category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℛ
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{R},\mathsf{Set}]
              consisting of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+functors"} regular functors
              only is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+class"} elementary class
              w.r.t. the signature given by (the underlying graph) of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℛ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
            %p
          %h2#stronger_conditions Stronger conditions
          %h3#exactness Exactness
          %p
            If a regular category additionally has the property that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence"} congruence
            is a kernel pair (and hence has a quotient), then it is called a (Barr-)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
            without being exact.
          %p
            Regularity and exactness can also be phrased in the language of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+connection"} Galois connection
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+kernels"} generalized kernels
          %h3#higher_arity Higher arity
          %p
            As exactness properties go, the ones possessed by general regular categories are fairly moderate; the main condition is of course stability of regular epis under pullback. A natural generalization is to include (finite or infinite) unions of subobjects, or equivalently images of (finite or infinite) families as well as of single morphisms. This leads to the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
          %p
            Just as regularity implies the existence of certain coequalizers, coherence implies the existence of certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushouts"} pushouts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lextensive+category"} lextensive category
            has all (finite or infinite) coproducts that are disjoint and stable under pullback. It is easy to see that a lextensive regular category must actually be coherent.
          %h2#the_regular_topology The regular topology
          %p
            Any regular category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcanonical+site"} subcanonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            whose covering families are generated by single
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphisms"} regular epimorphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+coverage"} regular coverage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact
            or has pullback-stable
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+coequalizer"} reflexive coequalizer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            for this topology (the necessary and sufficient condition is that any pullback of a kernel pair is again a kernel pair).
          %h2#making_categories_regular Making categories regular
          %p
            Any category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
            has a
            %strong reg/lex completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi reg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi lex
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{reg/lex}
            with the following properties:
          %ul
            %li
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ↪
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi reg
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi lex
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\hookrightarrow C_{reg/lex}
            %li
              Each object of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              becomes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi reg
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi lex
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{reg/lex}
            %li
              Each left-exact functor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is regular, extends to an essentially unique
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+functor"} regular functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mrow
                          %mi reg
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi lex
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{reg/lex}\to D
          %p
            In particular, the reg/lex completion is a left adjoint to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            from regular categories to lex categories (categories with finite limits). The reg/lex completion can be obtained by “formally adding images” for all morphisms in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            under images in its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+category"} presheaf category
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+and+exact+completions"} regular and exact completions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is regular,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi reg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi lex
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{reg/lex}
            is larger than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (that is, it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
            rather than merely a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion"} completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            satisfies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            (in the sense that all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphisms"} regular epimorphisms
            are split), then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi reg
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi lex
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\simeq C_{reg/lex}
          %p
            Regular categories of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi reg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi lex
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{reg/lex}
            for a lex category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            can be characterized as those regular categories in which every object admits both a regular epi from a projective object and a monomorphism into a projective object, and the projective objects are closed under finite limits. In this case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            can be recovered as the subcategory of projective objects. In fact, the construction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi reg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi lex
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{reg/lex}
            can be extended to categories having only
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+finite+limits"} weak finite limits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi reg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi lex
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{reg/lex}
            for a “weakly lex” category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            are those satisfying the first two conditions but not the third.
          %p
            When the reg/lex completion is followed by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ex%2Freg+completion"} ex/reg completion
            which completes a regular category into an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact one
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ex%2Flex+completion"} ex/lex completion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+and+exact+completions"} regular and exact completions
            for more about all of these operations.
